    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d503">

                <pb facs="0726" n="624" xml:id="pg624"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">765.84/731: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Geneva</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gilbert</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-08-03T11:00:00+01:00">August 3, 1935—11
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 11:48 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>265. I have been told by a member of the British delegation that London had
                    telephoned the delegation to the effect that consideration was being given there
                    to an approach to Washington on the subject of the United States collaborating
                    in some form with the projected conversations relating to the 1906
                    treaty—Consulate’s 262, August 2, 4 p.m.<anchor n="48" xml:id="d503-f1"/>
                    paragraph 1 (2)—having an observer present being mentioned as a possible
                    form.</p>
                <p>While I was told this in a manner exceedingly casual I gained the impression that
                    it was with a view to obtaining my reaction. I naturally made no comment
                    whatsoever.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gilbert</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
